In this lesson we'll look at linear modelling, which is just a fancy way of saying we use a straight line to describe something that changes at a constant rate. The idea is simple: when something changes by the same fixed amount every step, a linear model fits perfectly. You'll see the equation written as y equals m x plus b, where m is the change per step and b is where you start when x is zero. So really, m tells you the slope, and b tells you the starting value. You'll recognise linear situations everywhere: a phone plan with a flat monthly fee plus a per-minute charge, a taxi with a flag-fall plus a per-kilometre rate, simple interest where your money grows by the same dollar amount each year, or a car moving at constant speed. Speaking of simple interest, the formula is A equals the principal plus the principal times the rate times the number of periods, and because the interest added each year is fixed, it's linear in time. Now let's see it in action.